.\" Manual page for neko
.TH NEKO 1 "@MAN_DATE@" "Neko @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
neko \- portable spectral element flow solver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B neko
.I case-file
.br
.B mpirun
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.B neko
.I case-file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B neko
is a portable framework for high-order spectral element flow simulations.
It solves the incompressible (and compressible) Navier\-Stokes equations on
unstructured hexahedral meshes and supports execution on CPUs, NEC SX-Aurora
vector engines, NVIDIA GPUs (CUDA), AMD GPUs (HIP), Apple GPUs (Metal) and
OpenCL devices.
.PP
The simulation is driven by a single
.I case-file
that describes the mesh, the numerical scheme, boundary and initial
conditions, output, and any simulation components. The case file must have
either a
.B .case
or
.B .json
suffix; both are parsed as JSON.
.PP
.B neko
is parallelized with MPI and is normally launched through an MPI runtime such
as
.BR mpirun (1),
.BR mpiexec (1)
or a batch system launcher (e.g.
.BR srun (1)).
When built with OpenMP support, the number of threads per rank is controlled
via
.BR OMP_NUM_THREADS .
.SH GPU EXECUTION
When built for GPUs (CUDA, HIP, OpenCL or Metal),
.B neko
uses one device per MPI rank. Neko does
.B not
select or bind devices to ranks itself; it is the user's responsibility to
ensure that every rank is mapped to a single, distinct device. On multi-GPU
nodes this is normally arranged through the batch launcher together with a
small wrapper script that exposes only the node-local device to each rank,
e.g. by setting
.B CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES
(NVIDIA) or
.B ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES
(AMD) to the node-local rank id before starting the solver.
The Metal backend is the exception: it always uses the system default
device (Apple silicon exposes a single GPU), and no device mapping
applies.
.PP
A typical
.BR srun (1)
wrapper selects the visible device from the SLURM node-local rank:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#!/bin/bash
export ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=$SLURM_LOCALID
exec "$@"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Launching more ranks per node than there are devices, without restricting the
visible devices per rank, oversubscribes the GPUs and is generally not
supported.
.SS Zero\-copy mapping
On hardware with unified physical memory \(em Apple silicon (Metal) and
APUs such as the AMD Instinct MI300A with XNACK enabled (HIP) \(em mapped
arrays can alias the host allocation instead of keeping a separate
replicated device buffer, so host\-device transfers become no\-ops and the
memory footprint of mapped data is roughly halved. See
.B NEKO_METAL_ZEROCOPY
and
.B NEKO_HIP_ZEROCOPY
below; it is enabled by default on Apple silicon and opt\-in on HIP.
.PP
Since host and device then operate on the same allocation, host code
(e.g. user routines) must not write to a mapped array while device work
touching it may still be in flight; synchronize first.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I case-file
Path to a Neko case description, ending in
.B .case
or
.BR .json .
Any additional positional arguments are logged at startup but otherwise
ignored by the solver itself; they are available for user-supplied modules
linked in via
.BR makeneko (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NEKO_LOG_LEVEL
Verbosity of the logger. Higher values produce more output.
Common levels are
.BR 0 " (quiet), " 1 " (info), " 2 " (verbose), " 5
(log deprecated feature usage) and
.BR 10 " (debug)."
.TP
.B NEKO_LOG_FILE
If set, the log is also written to this file in addition to standard output.
.TP
.B NEKO_DEPRECATION_ERROR
If set to
.BR 1 ,
deprecated features whose configured removal version has been reached are
treated as fatal errors. If unset, they are reported as warnings.
.TP
.B NEKO_LOG_TAB_SIZE
Indentation width used by the logger.
.TP
.B NEKO_AUTOTUNE
Pins the kernel formulation used by the spectral element operators on the
CUDA and HIP backends, skipping the auto-tuning search that otherwise runs
on the first call of each operator. Accepted values are
.BR 1D " and " KSTEP .
When unset, each operator benchmarks both formulations, and the geometry
parameter of each, against the running case and caches the winner.
.TP
.B NEKO_EB_TUNE
Controls whether the auto-tuner sweeps the number of elements per thread
block for the kstep kernels. Enabled by default on CUDA, where blocking
measures a clear gain, and disabled on HIP, where the blocked kernels
exceed the register budget and spill to scratch. Set to
.BR 0 " or " 1
to override the per-backend default.
.TP
.B NEKO_EB
Selects the elements-per-block candidate used when the kstep formulation
is pinned with
.BR NEKO_AUTOTUNE=KSTEP .
Candidate
.B 0
is one element per block, i.e. the unblocked geometry. Values outside the
valid range fall back to
.BR 0 .
Defaults to
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B NEKO_CHUNKS
Selects the chunk size candidate used when the 1d formulation is pinned
with
.BR NEKO_AUTOTUNE=1D .
Candidates
.BR 0 " to " 3
are 1024, 512, 256 and 128 threads per block; candidate
.B 0
is the historical value. A candidate smaller than one derivative matrix
is invalid and falls back to 1024. Defaults to
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B NEKO_TUNE_ROUNDS
Number of interleaved sampling rounds used by the operator auto-tuner. Each
round times every candidate once \(em chunk sizes and elements per block
alike \(em and the best round per candidate is kept, so raising this guards
against transients and clock drift during the sweep. Defaults to
.BR 3 .
.TP
.B NEKO_TUNE_ITERS
Number of kernel launches timed per candidate per round, for both
sweeps. Lowering it
shortens startup on cases with very large element counts, at the cost of
noisier measurements. Defaults to
.BR 100 .
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_COMM
Selects the communication backend used by the gather\-scatter kernel.
Accepted values are
.BR MPI ", " MPIGPU ", " NCCL ", " SHMEM ", " CAF ", " UTOFU ", " NEIGHBOUR " and " MPIRMA ,
where
.B UTOFU
is native one\-sided communication over the Tofu interconnect,
.B NEIGHBOUR
is host MPI using an
.B MPI_Ineighbor_alltoallv
neighbourhood collective and
.B MPIRMA
is host MPI one\-sided communication into a passive\-target window.
When unset, a device\-aware variant is used
on accelerator builds; otherwise the host backends selected by
.B NEKO_GS_TUNE
are benchmarked at startup and the fastest one is used.
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_STRTGY
Selects the gather\-scatter strategy.
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_TUNE
Selects which communication backends are benchmarked at startup when
.B NEKO_GS_COMM
is unset. Takes a list of backend names, spelled as for
.BR NEKO_GS_COMM ,
separated by commas or spaces and matched case insensitively. Names
prefixed with
.B +
or
.B \-
are added to and removed from the default set, plain names replace it
altogether, and the two forms cannot be mixed. When unset, the default set
is every host backend the build supports except
.BR CAF ,
which has to be asked for with
.B NEKO_GS_TUNE=+CAF
since a compiler may accept coarrays at configure time and still give each
process a single image, which the coarray backend cannot communicate over.
.B MPIRMA
is benchmarked by default, but assumes the MPI implementation makes
one\-sided progress without the target entering MPI, which holds for
hardware\-driven components (Open MPI
.BR osc/rdma ", " osc/ucx ,
Cray MPICH over libfabric) but not for
.BR osc/pt2pt ;
skip it with
.B NEKO_GS_TUNE=\-MPIRMA
where it is known to be slow.
A list leaving a single candidate pins that backend without benchmarking.
Selecting a backend outright with
.B NEKO_GS_COMM
ignores this variable;
.B NEKO_GS_COMM=CAF
fails with an error if the run does not provide one image per rank.
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_CAF_SIGNALING
Selects the per\-pair synchronisation used by the coarray gather\-scatter
backend
.RB ( NEKO_GS_COMM=CAF ).
Accepted values are
.BR sync " (the default), " atomic ", " event " and " auto ,
where
.B auto
benchmarks the modes the build supports and binds the fastest one. The mode
is bound on the first gather\-scatter initialisation and cannot change
thereafter, so
.B auto
only takes effect when the communication backend is autotuned as well, that
is when
.B NEKO_GS_COMM
is unset and
.B CAF
is among the backends selected by
.BR NEKO_GS_TUNE ;
it falls back to
.B sync
otherwise.
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_UTOFU_NTNI
Number of Tofu network interfaces (TNIs) that the per\-thread injection
VCQs of the native Tofu (uTofu) gather\-scatter backend
.RB ( NEKO_GS_COMM=UTOFU )
are dealt over.
Defaults to
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_UTOFU_NVCQ
Caps the number of injection VCQs created by the uTofu gather\-scatter
backend (default: one per OpenMP thread). Useful when the hardware VCQ
budget, which is shared with the per\-instance receive VCQs, is tight.
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_UTOFU_NRVCQ
Number of receive VCQs per gather\-scatter instance path in the uTofu
backend (default: one per TNI). Each receive peer is bound to one, so
incoming halo traffic is spread over the TNIs;
.B 1
restores a single receive queue.
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_UTOFU_MASTER_INJECT
When set to a non\-zero value, the uTofu gather\-scatter backend issues
all puts from the master thread instead of thread\-parallel injection
(an A/B reference).
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_UTOFU_VEC
When set to
.BR 0 ,
disables the uTofu backend's fused vector (multi\-component) exchange;
multi\-component gather\-scatter falls back to independent scalar rounds.
.TP
.B NEKO_GS_RMA_FLUSH_ALL
When set to
.BR 0 ,
the MPI one\-sided gather\-scatter backend
.RB ( NEKO_GS_COMM=MPIRMA )
completes each peer's put with its own
.B MPI_Win_flush
instead of a single
.B MPI_Win_flush_all
for all of them (an A/B reference). Per\-peer flushing announces each
receiver as soon as its own put lands, at the cost of one flush call per
peer; it measured slower on Cray MPICH over Slingshot, hence the batched
default, but the balance depends on how cheaply the implementation scopes
a flush to one target.
.TP
.B NEKO_GLOBAL_INTERP_EL_FINDER
Selects the element search algorithm used by the global interpolation
routines.
.TP
.B NEKO_GLOBAL_INTERP_PE_FINDER
Selects the rank search algorithm used by the global interpolation routines.
.TP
.B NEKO_HIP_ZEROCOPY
When set to a non\-zero value, enables zero\-copy mapping of arrays in the
HIP backend: host and device share a single allocation instead of keeping
replicated copies, and host\-device transfers become no\-ops. This roughly
halves the memory footprint of mapped data, but is currently slower per
step than replicated buffers on MI300A, so it is an opt\-in capacity mode
for running larger cases rather than a speedup. Zero\-copy is only used on
an APU with unified physical memory and XNACK enabled
.RB ( HSA_XNACK=1 );
on a discrete GPU (e.g. MI250X, MI300X) a warning is printed and
replicated buffers are used. Disabled by default.
.TP
.B NEKO_METAL_ZEROCOPY
When set to
.BR 0 ,
disables zero\-copy mapping of arrays in the Metal backend. Enabled by
default on Apple silicon; on discrete GPUs without unified memory
replicated buffers are always used.
.TP
.B NEKO_MPI_THREAD_LEVEL
Overrides the MPI (and SHMEM) thread support level requested at startup.
Accepted values are
.BR single ", " funneled ", " serialized " and " multiple .
When unset, the level is chosen automatically:
.B MPI_THREAD_MULTIPLE
when running with more than one OpenMP thread per rank, falling back to
.B MPI_THREAD_FUNNELED
on host-only backends. Device backends require
.BR MPI_THREAD_MULTIPLE .
If the runtime cannot honor the requested level, initialization aborts.
.TP
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
Standard OpenMP variable controlling the number of threads per MPI rank when
.B neko
is built with OpenMP support.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGXCPU
Raised by the batch system when the CPU time limit is approaching. Neko
catches this signal and performs a clean shutdown at the next time step,
writing the final checkpoint and statistics.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Requests a graceful early termination. The current time step is completed
and the simulation finalizes normally.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Reserved for user-requested actions from within a simulation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The simulation completed successfully or was terminated cleanly by a
time-limit or user signal.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred during initialization, I/O, or the time-stepping loop.
A diagnostic message is written to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <case>.case
Primary case description (JSON syntax).
.TP
.I <case>.nmsh
Neko native mesh file referenced from the case.
.TP
.I <case>.fld , <case>.chkp
Field and checkpoint output written during the run; exact file names depend
on the
.B output
section of the case file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a case on a single rank:
.PP
.RS
.B neko tgv.case
.RE
.PP
Run with 8 MPI ranks:
.PP
.RS
.B mpirun -n 8 neko tgv.case
.RE
.PP
Hybrid MPI+OpenMP launch with 4 ranks and 8 threads each:
.PP
.RS
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS=8 mpirun -n 4 neko tgv.case
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR makeneko (1),
.BR mpirun (1),
.BR mpiexec (1),
.BR srun (1)
.PP
Project documentation and user guide:
.UR https://neko.cfd
.UE
.SH AUTHORS
Neko is developed by the Neko authors. See the
.B AUTHORS
file distributed with the source for a complete list of contributors.
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at
